{"context":"ADR-010 made the snapshot the transport between a repository and the\nworkspace canonical store: `\u003crepo\u003e/exchange/snapshots/` is an RSF\npackage in directory layout, committed with normal Git workflows. The\npackage carries six logical elements: the header, the manifest, the\ndeclarations, the integrity block, the unit entries and the\nattachments.\n\nOperational experience with team merges exposed a structural problem:\n**the manifest, the declarations and the integrity block are\nwhole-package projections.** Every knowledge change — even a change to\na single unit on a single side — rewrites them. A Git merge that\ncombined two pushed snapshots therefore always has these three files\ndiffering between the sides, regardless of whether the underlying\nknowledge changes touch different units. The result is a *synthetic*\nmerge conflict on derived state.\n\nResolving that conflict is impossible within Git's framework:\n\n- a **line-wise merge** combines lines, and the combined JSON is almost\n  always digest-inconsistent with the merged `units/` tree — the next\n  `eka sync pull` verifies the snapshot byte-exact and refuses it\n  (integrity failure, exit 1);\n- a **regenerating custom merge driver** cannot work: Git's `ort`\n  strategy invokes merge drivers **before** the merged source entries\n  are written to the index or the working tree, so a driver can never\n  observe the merge result it would regenerate from.\n\nThe only workable mitigation was a keep-ours driver plus an explicit\npost-merge repair command (`eka snapshot repair`) — functional, but it\nleaves a manual step in every team merge and keeps the synthetic\nconflict machinery alive for derived bytes that the store never even\nreads.","decision":"1. **The committed snapshot is SOURCE-ONLY.** The repository commits\n   `header.json` (the package contract: namespace, label, scope),\n   `units/\u003cns\u003e/\u003ctype\u003e-\u003cid\u003e-v\u003cn\u003e/unit.json` + `content`, and\n   `attachments/`. The derived aggregates — `manifest.json`,\n   `declarations.json`, `integrity.json` — are **gitignored**: they\n   are regenerated on every `eka sync push` and never enter version\n   control. The shipped `.gitignore` carries the three aggregate\n   paths.\n\n2. **Verification is per-unit and structural.** `exchange.LoadSnapshot`\n   selects the verification path by layout:\n   - a snapshot carrying the aggregates (written by EKA \u003c 0.10) is\n     verified **byte-exact** exactly as before\n     (`LoadPackageWithEntries`, package digest) — full backward\n     compatibility, including the idempotent-pull key;\n   - a source-only snapshot is verified structurally: header\n     strict-decoded (unknown fields rejected, RSF §9.5), every unit\n     strict-decoded with its directory matching its identity\n     (`UnitDirName`) and its content entry present, forms unique,\n     unknown entries rejected, attachments carried verbatim. The\n     per-unit digest is recomputed over `unit.json || content` (RSF\n     §9.4) — the same hash the canonical store content-addresses by —\n     so a corrupted unit can never be seeded silently.\n   - a partial aggregate state (exactly one of manifest/integrity\n     present) is a deterministic refusal (ambiguous).\n\n3. **The source fingerprint replaces the package digest as the\n   snapshot identity.** `exchange.SnapshotFingerprint` is SHA-256 over\n   the sorted (name, bytes) sequence of the source entries\n   (aggregates excluded defensively). It backs the idempotent-pull\n   check (`sync_log`), the `eka sync` report digest, and the\n   push-changed detection. Docs-mode pulls report the fingerprint of\n   the assembled package (`EmitSource`), so a docs pull followed by a\n   snapshot pull reports \"unchanged\" — the digests agree by\n   construction. Determinism is unchanged: identical source state\n   produces an identical fingerprint on every device.\n\n4. **Push emits the source projection.** `exchange.EmitSource`\n   projects the same deterministic bytes as `Emit`, minus the\n   aggregates. The single-file `.ekapkg` export/import path is\n   **unaffected**: `eka export` still produces the full package with\n   its integrity block.\n\n5. **`eka snapshot repair` becomes a legacy tool.** It regenerates the\n   aggregates only for snapshots that still carry them (EKA \u003c 0.10);\n   on a source-only snapshot it is a no-op normalizer (canonical\n   unit.json re-serialization + verification). The keep-ours\n   `.gitattributes` declaration is retained for legacy repositories\n   that still commit the aggregates.\n\n6. **Trust model.** The package-level digest covered *cross-entry\n   consistency* (manifest ↔ units). That consistency is now **by\n   construction**: the aggregates are derived from the units, never\n   read by the store. Per-unit integrity is preserved (recomputable\n   digest, content addressing in the store, `eka integrity check`),\n   and Git itself protects every committed file object. A tampered\n   unit.json (undecodable) is refused; a byte-level content change is\n   a new payload with a new hash, exactly as in the store model.","consequences":"- **Positive:** merging two pushed snapshots is a normal Git merge —\n  no synthetic conflicts on derived state, no post-merge repair step,\n  no per-machine driver registration for new snapshots.\n- **Positive:** the snapshot is smaller (three JSON files less per\n  snapshot), push writes less, diffs are purely knowledge-level.\n- **Positive:** full backward compatibility — legacy snapshots verify\n  byte-exact, legacy repositories keep the keep-ours driver + repair\n  workflow.\n- **Positive:** determinism, idempotency, cross-device byte-identity\n  and the store's content-addressing model are unchanged.\n- **Negative:** a package-level digest no longer travels with the\n  committed snapshot; cross-entry consistency is by construction\n  (aggregates are derived, never read), and per-unit integrity relies\n  on the recomputable per-unit digest + Git object integrity + the\n  store's content addressing.\n- **Negative:** a snapshot that still carries a partial aggregate\n  state (one file present) is refused until cleaned — a deterministic\n  edge, documented in the loader error.\n\n### References\n\n- [ADR-010 — Synchronization Model](adr-010-synchronization-model.md) — the snapshot transport this ADR amends (Decision 1: \"Knowledge Snapshot = RSF package in directory layout\")\n- [ADR-011 — Immutable Engineering Knowledge Model](adr-011-immutable-engineering-knowledge-model.md) — content addressing, per-unit hashes, store integrity\n- RSF §9 (determinism), §9.4 (digests), §9.5 (unknown fields) — [`../eka-reference-serialization-format-v1.1.md`](../eka-reference-serialization-format-v1.1.md)\n- Runbook: [`../../skeleton/docs/exchange/sync-conflicts.md`](../../skeleton/docs/exchange/sync-conflicts.md)","alternativesConsidered":"- **Keep the full package + keep-ours driver + `eka snapshot repair`**\n  (the 0.9.x mitigation) — rejected as the permanent model: leaves a\n  manual step in every team merge and maintains conflict machinery for\n  derived bytes.\n- **Custom regenerating merge driver** — rejected: Git's `ort`\n  strategy invokes drivers before the merged source entries exist in\n  the index or the working tree; a driver cannot observe the merge\n  result (empirically verified during the 0.9.x work).\n- **`merge=union` on the aggregates** — rejected: resolves silently to\n  an almost-always-invalid package that the next pull refuses, hiding\n  the problem behind a \"successful\" merge.\n- **Post-merge hook auto-regeneration** — rejected: ADR-010 explicitly\n  rejects hooks for synchronization behavior, and a hook cannot amend\n  the already-created merge commit cleanly.\n- **Splitting the manifest per unit** — rejected: dissolves the\n  package contract without adding value; the manifest is derived state\n  either way."}